1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,997
Programs need data, not prose.

2
00:00:03,217 --> 00:00:06,852
In Module 2 you asked for
a quiz as structured output.

3
00:00:07,072 --> 00:00:12,441
Now we go deeper: how to design a
schema, what structured outputs guarantee, what

4
00:00:12,441 --> 00:00:17,810
they don't, and how to handle every
kind of parsing failure with one clear error.

5
00:00:19,110 --> 00:00:22,633
You'll design Pydantic
schemas with useful constraints.

6
00:00:22,983 --> 00:00:26,565
Know what structured outputs
guarantee, and what they don't.

7
00:00:26,915 --> 00:00:31,336
Handle replies that are cut
off, invalid, refused, or invented.

8
00:00:31,686 --> 00:00:34,061
And check meaning in code, not just shape.

9
00:00:35,361 --> 00:00:38,760
A schema is a contract
between the model and your code.

10
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:44,882
The candidate profile has a name, years
of experience, a list of skills, and recent

11
00:00:44,882 --> 00:00:45,309
roles.

12
00:00:45,529 --> 00:00:48,396
Every field is
something code will actually use.

13
00:00:48,746 --> 00:00:50,313
Field adds constraints.

14
00:00:50,533 --> 00:00:53,771
Years of experience must
be between zero and sixty.

15
00:00:53,991 --> 00:00:57,076
A negative number is a
bug, and we want to catch it.

16
00:00:57,426 --> 00:01:00,712
A description tells the
model what belongs in a field.

17
00:01:00,932 --> 00:01:04,360
Here: technical skills
the resume names, as written.

18
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:08,372
Job requirements split skills
into must have and nice to have.

19
00:01:08,592 --> 00:01:11,548
That split is what makes
the comparison meaningful.

20
00:01:11,898 --> 00:01:16,080
And suggestions are a summary
plus one piece of advice per skill.

21
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:18,383
Notice there's no reasoning field.

22
00:01:18,603 --> 00:01:20,423
Ask only for what you'll use.

23
00:01:20,643 --> 00:01:23,790
Current models reason
before they answer anyway.

24
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:29,760
With structured outputs, the API
constrains the reply to your schema.

25
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:33,168
You get valid JSON, with
your fields and your types.

26
00:01:33,518 --> 00:01:38,739
Some constraints, like minimum and
maximum values, can't be enforced during

27
00:01:38,739 --> 00:01:39,505
generation.

28
00:01:39,725 --> 00:01:45,553
The SDK moves them into the field
description, and validates them with Pydantic when

29
00:01:45,553 --> 00:01:46,802
the reply arrives.

30
00:01:47,022 --> 00:01:49,557
If one fails, you get a validation error.

31
00:01:49,907 --> 00:01:52,001
And what's never guaranteed is truth.

32
00:01:52,221 --> 00:01:58,289
A perfectly valid reply can still contain
a wrong value, or a skill the resume never

33
00:01:58,289 --> 00:01:58,939
mentions.

34
00:02:00,239 --> 00:02:03,453
Let's see what Pydantic
reports for four replies.

35
00:02:03,673 --> 00:02:08,775
This needs no model at all: it's
the same validation the SDK runs.

36
00:02:08,995 --> 00:02:11,107
A good reply becomes an object.

37
00:02:11,327 --> 00:02:14,641
A negative number of years
fails greater than or equal.

38
00:02:14,861 --> 00:02:17,251
A missing field is reported as missing.

39
00:02:17,471 --> 00:02:22,208
And a reply cut off in the
middle isn't JSON at all: json invalid.

40
00:02:23,508 --> 00:02:27,125
Every model call in the
project goes through this one method.

41
00:02:27,345 --> 00:02:32,380
It calls parse with the schema as
output format, the system prompt, the rendered

42
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:34,268
template, and an effort level.

43
00:02:34,618 --> 00:02:37,595
If validation fails, we
look at the first error.

44
00:02:37,815 --> 00:02:43,699
Invalid JSON almost always means the
reply was cut off by max tokens, so we say

45
00:02:43,699 --> 00:02:44,072
that.

46
00:02:44,422 --> 00:02:48,993
Any other validation error names
the field and the problem, in one line.

47
00:02:49,343 --> 00:02:53,839
Then we record the call: which
prompt, its fingerprint, tokens, and cost.

48
00:02:54,189 --> 00:02:59,524
And we check the stop reason: a
refusal, or a reply that was cut off, never gets

49
00:02:59,524 --> 00:03:01,658
returned as if it were complete.

50
00:03:02,958 --> 00:03:04,945
Structured output checks the shape.

51
00:03:05,165 --> 00:03:06,696
Code can check meaning too.

52
00:03:06,916 --> 00:03:12,785
Mentions asks: does the resume text
actually name this skill, or one of its aliases?

53
00:03:13,135 --> 00:03:18,065
Grounded splits the extracted skills
into those the resume names, and those it

54
00:03:18,065 --> 00:03:18,570
doesn't.

55
00:03:18,790 --> 00:03:23,907
The analyzer keeps the first list, drops
the second, and tells you what it dropped.

56
00:03:25,207 --> 00:03:30,618
The project's fake API can misbehave on
purpose, so we can watch each failure path.

57
00:03:30,968 --> 00:03:35,853
A reply with negative years becomes
one clear error that names the field.

58
00:03:36,203 --> 00:03:40,387
A reply cut off mid-JSON:
not complete JSON, cut off.

59
00:03:40,737 --> 00:03:45,851
And when the reply invents a skill,
here COBOL, the grounding check drops it and

60
00:03:45,851 --> 00:03:46,427
tells us.

61
00:03:47,727 --> 00:03:48,534
Quick check.

62
00:03:48,754 --> 00:03:51,614
A structured output
reply passed validation.

63
00:03:51,834 --> 00:03:53,203
What do you actually know?

64
00:03:58,903 --> 00:04:01,000
That it has the right fields and types.

65
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:02,382
Shape, not truth.

66
00:04:02,602 --> 00:04:04,680
Where you can, check meaning in code.

67
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:10,774
A schema holds only the fields you
use, with constraints and descriptions.

68
00:04:11,124 --> 00:04:15,698
The API guarantees the shape,
and the SDK validates the limits.

69
00:04:16,048 --> 00:04:20,869
Cut-off, invalid, and refused
replies each become one clear error.

70
00:04:21,219 --> 00:04:25,161
And code checks meaning, like
grounding every skill in the resume.

71
00:04:25,511 --> 00:04:29,278
Next lesson: how do you know a
prompt change is an improvement?

72
00:04:29,498 --> 00:04:31,630
Prompt versions and evaluations.
